 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by matrix inversion of $4 \times 4$ ($\Sigma_{(t,d)}$) and $3 \times 3$ ($\Sigma_d$) matrices, which is $O(1)$ constant time per Gaussian. The refinement step involves MLP evaluation, also $O(1)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the full 7D parameters ($\mu_p, \mu_t, \mu_d, \Sigma$) plus refinement network weights. The conditional parameters are computed on-the-fly and do not need to be stored persistently [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by matrix inversion of $4 \times 4$ ($\Sigma_{(t,d)}$) and $3 \times 3$ ($\Sigma_d$) matrices, which is $O(1)$ constant time per Gaussian [G]. The refinement step involves MLP evaluation, also $O(1)$ [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the full 7D parameters ($\mu_p, \mu_t, \mu_d, \Sigma$) plus refinement network weights [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Efficiently integrates temporal and view-dependent effects into standard 3D rendering pipelines without modifying the core rasterizer [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The intrinsic shape of the Gaussian (covariance) remains static over time if refinement is not used, which may hinder representing complex non-rigid deformations [S1]. The refinement step adds computational overhead [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering dynamic scenes with view-dependent appearance using Gaussian Splatting.
-*   **When not to use:** For static scenes where view-dependence is handled by spherical harmonics alone, or for scenes with extremely rapid non-rigid deformations that exceed the capacity of the refinement MLP [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The intrinsic shape of the Gaussian (covariance) remains static over time if refinement is not used, which may hinder representing complex non-rigid deformations [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering dynamic scenes with view-dependent appearance using Gaussian Splatting [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Conditional Slicing Mechanism was introduced in "7DGS: Unified Spatial-Temporal-Angular Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. It extends the original 3D Gaussian Splatting by adding temporal and angular dimensions to the Gaussian representation. The method leverages standard properties of multivariate Gaussian distributions to condition the spatial component on observed time and view direction [S1]. It builds on the Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction method [8442-gaussian-geometry-parameters-prediction.md] by providing a mechanism to interpret the 7D parameters during rendering [S1].
